.page xxd 1 make a hex dump or turn one back into bytes

# SYNOPSIS
*xxd* [*-c* _COLS_] [*-g* _SIZE_] [*-l* _LEN_] [*-s* _SKIP_] [*-pu*] [_FILE_]
*xxd* *-r* [_FILE_]

# DESCRIPTION
`xxd` writes a hex dump of _FILE_ (standard input if there is none): the offset, the bytes in hexadecimal and the same bytes as ASCII text. With `-r` it goes the other way.

# OPTIONS
@ *-c* _COLS_
    Bytes per line. The default is 16.
@ *-g* _SIZE_
    Bytes per group, separated by a space. The default is 2; 0 puts no spaces between the bytes.
@ *-l* _LEN_
    Stop after _LEN_ bytes.
@ *-s* _SKIP_
    Start _SKIP_ bytes into the file (not for standard input).
@ *-p*
    Plain: just the hexadecimal digits, no offsets or text.
@ *-u*
    Use upper case hexadecimal digits.
@ *-r*
    Reverse: read hexadecimal digits and write the bytes they stand for. It understands plain dumps (`xxd -p`): it takes every pair of hexadecimal digits, skips a leading `offset:` and ignores everything else. A full dump with a text column must have that column cut off first.
@ *-i*
    Accepted and ignored.

# EXAMPLES
    echo -n abc | xxd -p
    echo 616263 | xxd -r -p
    xxd -l 32 /bin/hello
    xxd -c 8 -g 1 /Hakurei/etc/hostname

# SEE ALSO
hexdump(1), od(1), base64(1)
